  "module13": "What if you clicked?",
  "tip1": "Fraudsters target your feelings: urgency, fear, authority, curiosity and greed. If you suddenly feel pressure, that is exactly the moment to pause.",
  "tip2": "The display name proves nothing. Tap the sender and read the real address. Look for swapped letters and foreign domains.",
  "tip3": "Read an address from right to left: the part right before the first slash decides who owns the site. The padlock only means \"encrypted\", not \"safe\". Long-press links to see where they go.",
  "tip4": "Invoices are not programs. Beware of .exe, .lnk, .iso and macro files such as .xlsm, as well as double extensions and password-protected archives.",
  "tip5": "Bank details rarely change by email. Verify every change by phone on the known number. Even the boss has to follow the four-eyes principle.",
  "tip6": "Parcel, bank, \"Hi Mum\": these messages want you to click or pay. Open the app yourself or call the known number.",
  "tip7": "Voices can be cloned from a few seconds of audio and displayed numbers can be faked. Hang up and call back yourself on the known number.",
  "tip8": "You cannot see where a QR code leads. Check the address after scanning and look out for stickers over the original.",
  "tip9": "A separate password for every service, ideally from a password manager. Never approve a sign-in you did not start yourself.",
  "tip10": "The work laptop is for work only: no games, no mods, no private downloads. Public Wi-Fi only with VPN. Always lock it when you walk away.",
  "tip11": "Attackers read along: name, department and systems are enough for the perfect email. Share your successes, but no details about internal processes.",
  "tip12": "A hi-vis vest and full hands open many doors. Take strangers to reception in a friendly way. Found items like USB sticks go to IT, not into a computer.",
  "tip13": "It can happen to anyone. Disconnect the network and call IT at once. Every minute counts and nobody will judge you for it.",
